            <ArticleTitle>Transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulator for procedural pain associated with intravenous needlesticks.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>263-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Venipuncture continues to be considered a painful and unpleasant experience for those receiving medical treatment. A prospective study investigating whether the application of a transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulator (TENS) decreases the complaints of pain and unpleasantness with i.v. needle insertion was conducted using a group of 71 subjects who were double-blinded and randomized to one of three groups: TENS, placebo-TENS, and control. This article gives an overview of this research and describes its findings.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
